%N A398342 Numbers k, such that Sum_{d|k} rad(d) is a multiple of rad(k), where rad = A007947.
%C A398342 For odd primes p <= 59 the smallest term t that is a multiple of p has the property that any prime q < p is a divisor of p - 1. Does this hold for all odd primes p? - _David A. Corneth_, Sep 06 2026
%H A398342 David A. Corneth, <a href="/A398342/b398342.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..4032</a> (first 1200 terms from Žiga Pirc)
%e A398342 a(2) = 6 = 2*3; 6 is squarefree, thus rad(6) = 6, with divisors {1, 2, 3, 6}, all squarefree. The sum of divisors is 12, a multiple of 6. - _Michael De Vlieger_, Sep 01 2026
%e A398342 Prime factorization of 20 is 2^2*5, so rad(20) = 10. rad(1) + rad(2) + rad(4) + rad(5) + rad(10) + rad(20) = 30, so 3*10.
%t A398342 f[p_, e_] := (1 + e*p)/p; q[1] = True; q[k_] := IntegerQ[Times @@ f @@@ FactorInteger[k]]; Select[Range[6500], q] (* _Amiram Eldar_, Sep 01 2026 *)
%o A398342 (PARI) is_ok(n) = sumdiv(n, d, factorback(factorint(d)[, 1])) % factorback(factorint(n)[, 1]) == 0
